  {
    section: "Behaviour & Psychology",
    items: [
      {
        question: "Why do cats purr?",
        answer:
          "Cats purr for a variety of reasons, not just contentment. While a relaxed, purring cat curled in your lap is the classic image, cats also purr when they are anxious, in pain, or even giving birth. The vibration frequency of a purr (25–150 Hz) has been linked to promoting bone density and accelerating healing — so purring may be a built-in self-soothing mechanism as much as a social signal.",
      },
      {
        question: "Why does my cat knock things off tables?",
        answer:
          "This behaviour is a mix of curiosity, predatory instinct, and — let's be honest — attention-seeking. Cats use their paws to test whether objects are prey-like or safe to interact with. Pushing an item to watch it fall also provides sensory feedback and mental stimulation. If your cat does it while staring at you, congratulations: you have been successfully trained to react.",
      },
      {
        question: "What does slow blinking mean?",
        answer:
          "A slow blink from a cat is often called a \"cat kiss\" and signals trust and relaxed affection. When a cat slowly closes and reopens its eyes at you, it is communicating that it feels safe and comfortable in your presence. You can return the gesture — make eye contact, then slowly blink back. Many cats respond with a slow blink of their own.",
      },
      {
        question: "Why do cats bring dead animals as gifts?",
        answer:
          "Domestic cats retain the hunting instincts of their wild ancestors even when well-fed. Some cats, especially females, exhibit \"maternal teaching\" behaviour — bringing prey back to share with their social group (which now includes you). While the sentiment is endearing, the practical solution is a collar bell or a supervised outdoor schedule to reduce hunting.",
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    section: "Health & Care",
    items: [
      {
        question: "How often should I take my cat to the vet?",
        answer:
          "Healthy adult cats (1–10 years) should have a wellness check at least once a year. Senior cats (10+) benefit from bi-annual visits because age-related conditions like kidney disease, hyperthyroidism, and arthritis can progress quickly. Kittens follow a more intensive schedule for vaccinations and parasite prevention in the first year. Always visit sooner if you notice changes in appetite, litter-box habits, or behaviour.",
      },
      {
        question: "Are indoor cats healthier than outdoor cats?",
        answer:
          "Indoor cats typically live significantly longer — 12–18 years compared with 2–5 years on average for free-roaming outdoor cats. Outdoor cats face greater risks from traffic, predators, infectious disease, and toxin exposure. However, indoor cats need environmental enrichment (climbing structures, puzzle feeders, play sessions) to stay mentally stimulated and physically active. A supervised outdoor space or leash walks can offer the best of both worlds.",
      },
      {
        question: "What human foods are toxic to cats?",
        answer: `<strong>Always avoid:</strong> onions, garlic, leeks, and chives (damage red blood cells); grapes and raisins (can cause kidney failure); chocolate and caffeine (toxic to the nervous system); xylitol (found in some peanut butters and sugar-free products); alcohol; and raw dough containing yeast. Dairy is often over-stated as a risk — most adult cats are lactose intolerant and may experience digestive upset, but it is rarely dangerous in tiny amounts. When in doubt, stick to species-appropriate cat food.`,
      },
      {
        question: "How do I know if my cat is at a healthy weight?",
        answer:
          "The easiest at-home check is the rib test: run your fingers gently along your cat's sides. You should be able to feel the ribs easily without pressing hard, but they shouldn't be visually protruding. Looking from above, a healthy cat has a slight waist tuck behind the ribs. Your vet uses a Body Condition Score (BCS) chart on a 1–9 scale; a score of 4–5 is ideal for most cats.",
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    section: "Breeds & History",
    items: [
      {
        question: "When were cats domesticated?",
        answer:
          "Archaeological and genetic evidence points to cats beginning their relationship with humans around 10,000 years ago in the Fertile Crescent, coinciding with the rise of agriculture. Grain stores attracted rodents, which attracted wildcats (Felis silvestris lybica) — the sole wild ancestor of all domestic cats. Unlike dogs, cats essentially domesticated themselves by proving useful, and they remain genetically very close to their wild ancestors.",
      },
      {
        question: "What is the difference between a breed and a moggy?",
        answer:
          "A \"moggy\" (or domestic shorthair/longhair) is a cat of mixed or unknown ancestry — the feline equivalent of a mixed-breed dog. Pedigree or purebred cats belong to a recognised breed with a documented lineage and breed standard defining appearance and temperament. Moggies make up the vast majority of pet cats worldwide and tend to exhibit greater genetic diversity, which can confer health advantages such as a reduced likelihood of hereditary conditions.",
      },
      {
        question: "Are orange cats really more friendly?",
        answer:
          "There is some evidence that coat colour and personality are loosely correlated via the genes that influence both pigmentation and behaviour. Orange (red) colouration is linked to the X chromosome, and roughly 80% of orange cats are male. Anecdotally, orange cats have a reputation for being bold and sociable, and a 2015 UC Berkeley survey found owners rated orange cats as friendlier than white or tortoiseshell cats. That said, individual upbringing and socialisation play a far bigger role.",
      },
    ],
  },
];

// ── Component helpers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

function createIcon() {
  const svg = document.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "svg");
  svg.setAttribute("width", "14");
  svg.setAttribute("height", "14");
  svg.setAttribute("viewBox", "0 0 14 14");
  svg.setAttribute("fill", "none");
  svg.setAttribute("stroke", "currentColor");
  svg.setAttribute("stroke-width", "2.2");
  svg.setAttribute("stroke-linecap", "round");

  const h = document.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "line");
  h.setAttribute("x1", "2"); h.setAttribute("y1", "7");
  h.setAttribute("x2", "12"); h.setAttribute("y2", "7");

  const v = document.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "line");
  v.setAttribute("x1", "7"); v.setAttribute("y1", "2");
  v.setAttribute("x2", "7"); v.setAttribute("y2", "12");

  svg.appendChild(h);
  svg.appendChild(v);
  return svg;
}

// ── FAQItem component ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

function FAQItem({ question, answer }) {
  // Outer wrapper — required class: "example"
  const wrapper = document.createElement("div");
  wrapper.className = "example";

  // Trigger button
  const trigger = document.createElement("button");
  trigger.className = "faq-trigger";
  trigger.setAttribute("aria-expanded", "false");

  const questionText = document.createElement("span");
  questionText.className = "question-text";
  questionText.textContent = question;

  const iconWrap = document.createElement("span");
  iconWrap.className = "faq-icon";
  iconWrap.setAttribute("aria-hidden", "true");
  iconWrap.appendChild(createIcon());

  trigger.appendChild(questionText);
  trigger.appendChild(iconWrap);

  // Controlled content — required class: "details"
  const details = document.createElement("div");
  details.className = "details";

  const inner = document.createElement("div");
  inner.className = "details-inner";

  const body = document.createElement("div");
  body.className = "details-body";
  body.innerHTML = `<p>${answer}</p>`;

  inner.appendChild(body);
  details.appendChild(inner);

  wrapper.appendChild(trigger);
  wrapper.appendChild(details);

  // Toggle behaviour
  trigger.addEventListener("click", () => {
    const isOpen = wrapper.classList.toggle("open");
    trigger.setAttribute("aria-expanded", String(isOpen));
  });

  return wrapper;
}

// ── FAQSection component ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

function FAQSection({ section, items }) {
  const fragment = document.createDocumentFragment();

  const label = document.createElement("p");
  label.className = "faq-section-label";
  label.textContent = section;
  fragment.appendChild(label);

  items.forEach((item) => {
    fragment.appendChild(FAQItem(item));
  });

  return fragment;
}

// ── App mount ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

function mountApp() {
  const container = document.getElementById("faq-root");
  if (!container) return;

  faqs.forEach(({ section, items }) => {
    container.appendChild(FAQSection({ section, items }));
  });
}

document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", mountApp);
